<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <!--
      The grain-backed half of the sending domain's tests, one class per failure class docs/plan/17
      § The parts that are actually the work names: the same idempotency key sending once across a
      deactivation and a different one sending twice, the suppressed address the provider is never
      told about, STOP arriving inbound and the next send being refused, the runaway loop stopping at
      the spend limit with the limit and the window named, the delivery receipt for a provider
      message id nobody remembers being ignored rather than fatal, and the template whose missing
      parameter fails before the carrier.

      ⚠ NO Testcontainers AND NO Aspire.Hosting, AND THAT IS THE SAME DEVIATION FROM ADR-018 THAT
      CyberCloud.Metering.Tests AND CyberCloud.ResourceManager.Tests RECORD, OWED BACK ON THE SAME
      TERMS. docs/plan/02 ADR-018 wants grain behaviour tested "against a real in-process TestCluster
      … with real storage providers backed by Testcontainers". This project uses Orleans.TestingHost's
      TestCluster with AddMemoryGrainStorage instead, because the environment this was built in could
      not start a container.

      What that costs, stated so it can be paid rather than forgotten:

        - Nothing here proves the state SERIALIZES. In-memory storage keeps the object graph; a real
          provider round-trips it. SuppressionListState.Entries is a Dictionary and
          CommunicationServiceState.Channels a List, both `{ get; set; }` for exactly the "STJ does
          not populate a get-only collection" trap — but the assertion that they round-trip is owed,
          and for the suppression list it is the assertion that matters most: a list that fails to
          deserialize is a list that has silently forgotten every opt-out, which is the one failure
          in this module with a legal consequence.
        - Nothing here proves the DURABLE tier is durable across a silo restart. Deactivation is used
          to exercise re-activation, which keeps the same in-process store — so
          "IdempotencySurvivesDeactivation" proves the grain re-reads its state rather than proving
          Redis kept it.
        - Nothing here exercises a real carrier, by design. IChannelProvider is the seam and
          InMemoryChannelProvider is the double; what is proven is our side of it, which is where
          every property docs/plan/17 asks for actually lives.
    -->

    <ItemGroup>
        <PackageReference Include="xunit.v3"/>
        <PackageReference Include="Shouldly"/>
        <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Orleans.TestingHost"/>
        <PackageReference Include="Orleans.Multitenant"/>
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup Label="Global usings">
        <Using Include="Xunit"/>
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <ProjectReference Include="../CyberCloud.Communication/CyberCloud.Communication.csproj"/>
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
